#v(0.8em)

As the wife is to know #emph[her] duty, so the husband #footnote[Husbands bound to duty. #emph[Propterea debent mulieribus suis ad virtutis exemplum se praebere, quia viri sunt. Aug. de adult. Coniug. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 8.] much more #emph[his,] because he is to be a guide, and good example to his wife, he is to dwell with her #emph[according to knowledge,] (1 Pet. 3. 7.) the more eminent his place is, the more knowledge he ought to have how to walk worthy thereof. Neglect of duty in him is more dishonourable unto God, because by virtue of his place he is #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 11. 7.] #emph[the Imag eand glory of God,] and more pernicious not to his wife only, but also to the whole family, because of that power and authority he hath, which he may abuse to the maintenance of his wickedness, having in the house no superior power to restrain his fury: whereas the wife, though never so wicked, may by the power of her husband be kept under, and restrained from outrage.

Wherefore to go on in order, in laying down the husbands #footnote[Husbands particular duties.] duties (as we have the wives) we are to consider,

- 1\. The #emph[duties] themselves.
- 2\. The #emph[reasons] to enforce them.

In setting down the duties we must note

- 1\. The #emph[matter] wherein they consist.
- 2\. The #emph[manner] how they are to be performed.

The Apostle compriseth the whole #emph[matter] of them all under #emph[love,] which is the #emph[sum] and #emph[head] of all.

This we will first handle: and then proceed to other particulars.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of that] #footnote[Treat. 3. §. 2. 1. Husbands must love their wives.] #emph[love which husbands owe their wives.]

This head of all the rest, #emph[love,] is expressly set down, and alone mentioned in this, and in many other places of Scripture, whereby it is evident, that all other duties are comprised under it.

To omit other places, where this duty is urged in #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 25, 28, 33.] this place, #emph[love] is four times by name expressed, beside that it is intimated under many other terms and phrases.

Whosoever therefore taketh a wife, must, in this respect that she is his wife, love her: as it is noted of #emph[Isaac] (the best pattern of husbands noted in the Scripture) #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 24. 67.] #emph[he took Rebekah, she was his wife ana he loved her.]

Many good reasons hereof may be rendered. #footnote[No duty well performed without love.]

1. Because no duty on the husbands part can be rightly performed except it be seasoned with love. The Apostle exhorteth all Christians to #emph[do all their things in love:] much more #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 16. 14.] ought husbands: though in place they be above their wives, yet love may not be forgotten.

2. Because of all persons on earth a wife is the most proper #footnote[2\. A wife the most proper object of love. #emph[Deut.] 13. 6.] object of love: nor friend, nor child, nor parent ought so to be loved as a wife: she is termed, #emph[the wife of his bosom,] to show that she ought to be as his heart in his bosom.

3. Because his place of eminency, and power of authority #footnote[3\. Love keeps a man from abusing his authority.] may soon puff him up, and make him insult over his wife, and trample her under his feet, if a entire #emph[love] of her be not planted in his heart. To keep him from abusing his authority is #emph[love] so much pressed upon him.

4. Because wives through the weakness of their sex (for #footnote[4\. Love maketh him bear with her infirmities. 5. His love draweth love from her.] they are the weaker vessels) are much prone to provoke their husbands. So as if there be not love predominant in the husband, there is like to be but little peace betwixt man and wife. #emph[love covereth a multitude of imperfections.]

5. Because as Christ by his love first manifested provoketh the Church to love him, so an husband by loving his wife should provoke her to love him again: showing himself like the Sun which is the fountain of light, and from which the Moon receiveth what light she hath: so he should be the fountain of love to his wife.

#emph[Object. Love] was before laid down as a common duty appertaining both to man and wife: how is it then here required as a particular and peculiar duty of an husband?

#emph[Answ.] In regard of the general extent of love it is indeed a common duty belonging to the one as well as to the other, yea #footnote[How #emph[love] is an husbands particular duty. 1 #emph[Cor.] 13. 5.] belonging to all Christians, to all men: for it is the very nature of love, and an especial property thereof, #emph[to seek not her own things,] but the good of others, which all are bound to do by virtue of the bond of nature; more then others, Christians by virtue of the bond of the spirit: among Christians, especially wives and husbands by virtue of the matrimonial bond: of married couples, most of all husbands by virtue of their place and charge. Their place is a place of #emph[authority,] which without love will soon turn into #emph[tyranny.] Their charge is especially and above all, to seek the good of their wives: as wives are the chiefest, and greatest charge of husbands, so their chiefest and greatest care must be for them: the parents and friends of wives as they give over all their authority to their husbands, so they cast all care upon them: wherefore that husbands may take the more care of their wives, and the better seek their good, they ought after a peculiar manner to love them. Husbands are most of all bound to love: and bound to love their wives most of all.

Thus this affection of #emph[love] is a distinct duty in it self, peculiarly #footnote[Love a common condition to be annexed to every duty.] appertaining to an husband: and also a common condition which must be annexed to every other duty of an husband, to season and sweeten the same. His look, his speech, his carriage, and all his actions, wherein he hath to do with his wife, must be seasoned with love: love must show it self in his commandments, in his reproofs, in his instructions, in his admonitions, in his authority, in his familiarity, when they are alone together, when they are in company before others, in civil affairs, in religious matters, at all times, in all things: as salt must be first and last upon the table, and eaten with every bit of meat, so must love be first in an husbands heart, and last out of it, and mixed with every thing wherein he hath to do with his wife.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of an husbands hatred and want of love.]

Contrary hereunto is hatred of heart: which vice as it is #footnote[Contrary, 1. Hatred.] very odious and detestable in it self, so much more when the wife is made the object thereof. As love provoketh an husband to do his wife what good he can, so hatred, to do her what mischief he can. #emph[Moses] noteth a mans hatred of his #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 22. 13, #emph[etc.]] wife to be a cause of much mischief: for the nearer, and dearer any persons be, the more violent will that hatred be which is fastened on them.

Hence was it that a divorce was suffered to be made betwixt #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 24. 3.] a man and his wife, in case he hated her: which law questionless was made for relief of the wife, lest the hatred which her husband conceived against her should work her some mischief, if he were forced to keep her as his wife: which Christ seemeth to imply in these words, #emph[Moses, because of the] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 8.] #emph[hardness of your hearts, suffered you to put away your wines.] This therefore being so pestilent a poison, let husbands take heed how they suffer it to soak into them.

Neither is it sufficient for an husband not to hate his wife, #footnote[2\. Want of love.] for even the #emph[want of love,] though it be only a privation, yet is it a great vice, and contrary also to the forenamed duty of love. Where this #emph[want of love] is, there can be no duty well performed, even as when the great wheel of a clock, the first mover of all the rest, is out of frame, never a wheel can be in good order. They that think lightly hereof, plainly discover that there is little or no love of God in them at all: for if the Apostles inference be good, taken from a mans neighbour or brother whom #footnote[1 #emph[Joh.] 4. 20.] he hath seen, it will much more be good having relation to a wife: for #emph[how can he who loveth not his wife,] (whom God hath given to him as a token of his favour, and as an help meet for him, to be in his bosom, and ever in his sight, yea to be no more two, but one flesh) #emph[love God whom he hath not seen? If any man saith, he loveth God, and hate his wife, he is a lier.] Let husbands therefore by loving their wives give evidence that they love God.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of an husbands] #footnote[Treat. 3. §. 9. Branches of love.] #emph[wise maintaining his authority.]

All the branches which grow out of this root of #emph[love,] as they have respect to husbands duties, may be drawn to two heads:

- 1\. #emph[A wise maintaining of his authority.]
- 2\. #emph[A right managing of the same.]

That these two are branches of an husbands #emph[love,] is evident by the place wherein God hath set him, which is a #emph[place] of #emph[authority;] for the best good that any can do, and so the best fruits of love which he can show forth to any, are such as are done in his own proper place, and by virtue thereof. If then an husband relinquish his authority, he disableth himself from doing that good, and showing those fruits of love which otherwise he might. If he abuse his authority, he turneth the edge and point of his sword amiss: in stead of holding it over his wife for her protection, he turneth it into her bowels to her destruction, and so manifesteth thereby more hatred then love.

Now then to handle these two severally, and distinctly:

I. That an husband ought wisely to #emph[maintain] his authority, #footnote[II. Husbands must maintain their authority.] is implied under this Apostolicall precept, #emph[husbands dwell with your wives according to knowledge,] that is, as such as are well able to maintain the honour of that place wherein God #footnote[1 #emph[Pet.] 3. 7..] hath set you: not as sots and fools without understanding. The same is also implied under the titles of preeminence which the Scripture attributeth to husbands, as #emph[Lord, master,] #footnote[See Treat. 3. §. 3. Reasons.] #emph[head, guide, image and glory of God, etc.]

The honour and authority of God, and of his son Christ #footnote[1\. Gods authority maintained by the husbands.] Jesus, is maintained in and by the honour and authority of an husband, as the Kings authority is maintained by the authority of his Privy Council and other Magistrates under him; yea, as an husbands authority is in the family maintained by the authority of his wife: (for as #emph[the man is the glory of God, so] #footnote[1 Cor. 11. 7.] #emph[the woman is the glory of the man.)]

The good of the wife her self is thus also much promoted, #footnote[2\. The wives good promoted thereby.] even as the good of the body is helped forward by the heads abiding in his place; should the head be put under any of the parts of the body, the body and all the parts thereof could not but receive much damage thereby: even so the wife and whole family would feel the damage of the husbands loss of his authority.

#strong[1. #emph[Quest.] Is it in the power of the husband to maintain his own authority?]

#emph[Answ.] Yea, in his more then in any others: for note the #footnote[It is in the mans power to maintain his authority. 1 #emph[Tim.] 4. 12.] counsel of the Apostle to #emph[Timothy,] (though in another case, yet very pertinent to this purpose) #emph[Let no man despise thy youth.] It was therefore in #emph[Timothies] power to maintain his honour, and not to suffer it to be despised; and so is it in an husbands power.

#strong[2. #emph[Quest.] How may an husband best maintain his authority?]

#emph[Answ.] That direction which the Apostle giveth to #emph[Timothy] #footnote[How a mans authority may be well maintained. #emph[Ad viros pertinet virtute vincere, & exemplo regere foeminae. Aug. de Adult. Coniug. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 8.] to maintain his authority, may fitly be applied for this purpose unto an husband; #emph[Be an ensample in conversation, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in pureness:] as if he had said, If thou walk before them worthy of thy place and calling, and worthy of that honour and respect which is due thereunto, showing forth the fruits of love, faith, and other like graces, assuredly they will reverence thy youth; but if otherwise thou carry thy self basely, and not beseeming a minister, thou givest them just occasion to despise thee. Even thus may husbands best maintain their authority by being an ensample in love, gravity, piety, honesty, etc. The fruits of these and other like graces showed forth by husbands before their wives and family, cannot but work a reverend and dutiful respect in their wives and whole house towards them: for by this means they shall more clearly discern the image of God shine forth in their faces.

#emph[Object.] Very goodness and grace it self is hated of wicked and ungodly wives: it was an act of piety that made #emph[Michal] despise #emph[David.]

#emph[Answ.] 1. Grant it to be so: yet this may be a good direction for such husbands as have not such wicked wives.

2. This doth not always so fall our, no, nor yet for the most part in those that are wicked; true virtue and integrity doth oft cause admiration in such as love it not.

3. Though some be of so crooked and perverse a disposition as to take occasion of contempt, where none is given, yet shall that husband justify himself before God and man, that carryeth himself worthy of his place.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of husbands losing their authority.]

Contrary is their practise who by their profaneness, riotousness, #footnote[Contrary, 1. base carriage] drunkenness, lewdness, lightness, unthriftiness, and other like base carriage, make themselves contemptible, and so lose their authority: though a wife ought not to take these occasions to despise her husband, yet is it a just judgment on him to be despised, seeing he maketh himself contemptible.

Contrary also to the forenamed directions is the sterne, #footnote[2\. Cruel dealing.] rough, and cruel carriage of husbands, who by violence and tyranny go about to maintain their authority. Force may indeed cause fear, but a slavish feat, such a fear as breedeth more hatred then love, more inward contempt, then outward respect.

And contrary is their servile disposition, who against their #footnote[3\. Yielding in unlawful things.] own judgment yield to the bent of their wives mind in such things as are unlawful: they will lose their authority rather then give discontent to their wives: which is a fault expressly forbidden by the #footnote[#emph[Dent.] 13. 6, 7.] law: and yet a fault whereinto not only wicked #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 21. 7, 9.] #emph[Ahab,] but also wise #emph[Solomon] fell: how heinous a fault, and how grievous a fall this was in #emph[Solomon,] the fearful issue thereof showeth. Like to him not in wisdom, but in this point of egregious folly, are such as upon their wives #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 11. 4.] instigation, suffer Priests and Jesuits, to lurk and celebrate Masses in their houses, and yield to be present thereat themselves. Like to #emph[Ahab] are such Magistrates as suffer their wives to oversway them in course of Justice: hence it cometh to pass that more petitions and suites are made to the wives of Magistrates in the cases of Justice then to the Magistrates themselves: and the favour of their wives is more esteemed then their own: so as the power of governing, and the main stroke in determining matters, is from their wives; they are but the mouths and instrument of their wives, in so much as among the common people the title of their places and offices is given to their wives. Some husbands suffer this by reason of their fearful, and foolish disposition, wanting courage and wisdom to maintain the honour of their places against the insolency of their wives: others upon a subtil, covetous, wicked mind, that by the means of their wives there may be more freedom for receiving bribes. Among these I may reckon those who against their own mind, to satisfy their wives mind, suffer both wives and children to follow the fashion, to attire themselves unbeseeming their places, to frequent light company, with the like; and also those who upon their wives importunity are moved (as #emph[Samson] was) to reveal such secrets #footnote[#emph[Maximè obseruate vos atque attendite anim bus vestris ô viri ab vxoris concitationibus atque malis instigationibus, per hanc enim in vltimo supplantare Job visus est diabolus, etc. per istas etiam nunc plurimos subuertit. Orig. in Ioh. lib.] 2.] as are not meet to be known. Husbands may hearken to their wives moving good things, but they may not obey them in evil things: if they do, their fault is double: 1. In doing evil: 2. In losing their authority.

Let husbands therefore be very watchful against their wives evil instigations. Satan laboured to supplant #emph[Job] by his wife: and by this doth he subvert many in these days.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of husbands] #footnote[Treat 3. §. 3. How a mans authority may be well managed.] #emph[high account of wives.]

As authority must be well #emph[maintained,] so must it be well #emph[managed:] for which purpose two things are needful:

- 1\. That an husband tenderly respect his wife.
- 2\. That providently he care for her.

An husbands tender respect of his wife is

+ #emph[Inward.]
+ #emph[Outward.]

#emph[Inward] in regard of his

+ #emph[Opinion] of her.
+ #emph[Affection] to her.

Outward in regard of his #emph[carriage] towards her.

For an husbands #emph[opinion] of his wife, two things are to be weighed

+ Her #emph[place.]
+ Her #emph[person.]

1. Her #emph[place] is indeed a place of inferiority, and subjection, yet the nearest to equality that may be: a place of common #footnote[Husbands must account their wives yoke-fellows. 1 #emph[Pet.] 3. 7.] equity in many respects, wherein man and wife are after a sort even fellows, and partners: Hence then it followeth that

#emph[The husband must account his wife a yoke-fellow and companion.] This is one point of #emph[giving honour] to the wife: and it is implied under that phrase whereby the #emph[end] of making a wife is noted, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 18.] which in our English is translated, #emph[meet for him,] word for word #emph[as before him] that is, like himself, one in whom he might #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 28..] see himself, or even (to use our Apostles word) #emph[himself.] These phrases imply a kind of fellowship: as also the many prerogatives that are common to both, which have been noted #footnote[Treat. 3. §. 4.] before.

As a wives acknowledgment of her husbands superiority is the ground-work of all her duties, so an husbands acknowledgment of that fellowship which is betwixt him and his wife, will make him carry himself much more amiably, familiarly, lovingly, and every way as beseemeth a good husband towards her.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of that fellowship which is betwixt man and wife, notwithstanding a wives inferiority.]

#strong[#emph[Object. Fellowship] betwixt man and wife cannot stand with a wives #emph[inferiority] and #emph[subjection.]]

#emph[Answ.] They are of very mean capacity that cannot see how these may stand together. Is there not a fellowship betwixt superior and inferior magistrates in relation to their subjects? Yea the Scripture mentioneth a fellowship betwixt Christ the head and other Saints in relation to the glory #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 8. 17..] whereof all are made partakers (for it termeth us #emph[joint heirs with Christ:)] and in relation to Gods people a fellowship betwixt God and his ministers (for it termeth them #emph[labourers together] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 3. 9..] #emph[with God)] yet none can deny the Saints, and ministers to be inferior and in subjection to Christ, and God. But distinctly to answer the objection.

1. There may not only be a fellowship, but also an equality in some things betwixt those that in other things are one of them inferior and subject: as betwixt man and wife in the power of one another bodies: for #emph[the wife] (as well as the husband) #footnote[#emph[Vxor & serua & domina est viri, Chrysost. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] is therein #emph[both a servant, and a mistress, a servant to yield her body, a mistress to have the power of his.]

2. There may be #emph[fellowship] in the very same things wherein is #emph[inferiority:] for #emph[fellowship] hath respect to the thing it self, #emph[inferiority] to the measure, and manner: as in #emph[giving light] the Sun and Moon have a fellowship, but in the measure and manner the Moon is inferior: the Moon hath not so much light as the Sun, and that which it hath it hath from the Sun: and as in #emph[governing,] the king and other magistrates have a fellowship, but in the measure, and manner of government they are inferior to him: Even so is it betwixt man and wife, in many things wherein there is a fellowship, the wife is notwithstanding inferior: so as inferiority may stand with fellowship.

3. There are no unequals betwixt which there is so near a parity as betwixt man and wife: if therefore there may be a fellowship betwixt any that are superior, and inferior one to another, then much more betwixt man and wife.

#footnote[Debet in vxorem vir dominari, vt animus in corpus, consentiens ei, & conspirans beneuolentia. Plut. praec. connub.] As the soul therefore ruleth over the body, by a mutual and loving consent and agreement, so must a man over his wife.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of husbands too mean account of wives.]

Contrary is the conceit of many who think there is no #footnote[#emph[Non es dominus sed maritus: non ancillam sortitus es, sed vxorem: gubernatorem te Deus voluit esse sexus inferioris, non praepotentem. Ambr. Hexaem. lib.] 5. #emph[cap.] 7.] difference betwixt a wife and servant but in familiarity: and that wives were made to be servants to their husbands, because subjection, fear and obedience are required of them: whence it cometh to pass that wives are oft used little better then servants. A conceit and practise savouring too much of heathenish, and sottish arrogancy. Did God at first take the wife out of mans side, that man should tread her under his feet? Or rather that he should set her at his side next to him above all children, servants, or any other in the family, how near, or dear unto him soever? For none can be nearer then a wife, and none ought to be dearer.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of husbands good esteem of their] #footnote[Treat 3. §. 5. IIII. Husbands must account their own wives best for them.] #emph[own wives.]

2. For the #emph[person] of a wife, #emph[An husband ought to esteem that particular person to whom by Gods providence he is joined in marriage, to be the fittest, and best for him.] This is implied under that particle of restraint (OWN) noted by the Apostle where he saith #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 25.] #emph[husbands love your] OWN #emph[wives,] and again presseth it under a comparison of the body #emph[(as] #footnote[#emph[Ves.] 28.] #emph[your] OWN #emph[bodies)] Every one thinketh his own body best and fittest for him. A man might happily wish some defects or enormities in his own body to be amended, and desire that his were like an others, more strait, strong, and comely then his own, yet would he not have his head to be upon that other mans body: the same opinion ought a man (that would love his wife) to have of her.

Good reason there is for him so to do: for true is the proverb, #footnote[#emph[Reason.] Every ones wife is provided by God.] if it be rightly taken, #emph[marriages are first made in heaven,] that is, God hath an over-ruling hand in ordering them: which #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 19. 14.] #emph[Solomon] implieth by that opposition which he maketh betwixt wealth and a wife: #emph[that] is from our fathers, #emph[this] from the Lord: in which respect he saith, #footnote[#emph[&] 18. 22.] #emph[he which findeth a wife receiveth favour of the Lord.] If therefore thou art loved of God, and lovest him, he will make thy wife prove a good thing to thee.

#strong[#emph[Object.] A wife may be a very lewd and wicked woman: how then can she be accounted the best wife?]

#emph[Answ.] 1. It may be she was good enough when first she was brought to thee, but thou by thine evil example, or negligent government, or hard usage, hast made her so bad as she is. Which if it be so, then is she to be considered not as thou hast marr'd her, but as thou diddest marry her.

2. Though she be not in relation to other wives the best in condition, yet in relation to thee she may be the best in event: if not for thine ease and quiet, yet for trial of thy wisdom and patience: and so as #footnote[#emph[Socrates interrogatus qua gratia vxoris mores toleraret, respondit, vt gymnasium & palaestram philosophiae domi habeam. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 11.] a school of virtue she may be unto thee. As #footnote[#emph[Ars tua Tiphy jacet si non sit in aequore fluctus Ovid. de Trist. l.] 4. #emph[eleg.] 3.] a skilful pilots sufficiency is tried and known by tempestuous seas, so a mans wisdom by a troublesome wife. Yea she may be given thee as a punishment of some former sins, as seeking after a beautiful, honourable, rich, proper wife, rather then a religious and honest one: or seeking her without any direction or help first sought of God, or otherwise then thou hast warrant from God, as by stealth, and without parents consent; or some other sins in another kind, to bring thee to repentance: or as a means to restrain and wean thee from some future sins whereunto thou are subject, and so prove a blessed cross to keep thee from a fearful curse.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of husbands preposterous opinion of their own wives.]

Contrary is a corrupt and perverse opinion which many have of their #emph[own] wives, thinking them of all other the worst and unfittest; yea though they be such as every way both in gifts and qualities of mind, and also in grace and comeliness of body deserve all good respect and esteem. Whereas #emph[others] (which look with a single eye) commend their good parts, #emph[they] Misinterpret and misjudge all: if their wives be religious, they think them #emph[hypocrites:] if grave, sober and modest, #emph[melancholicke:] if cheerful, #emph[wanton:] if they keep at home, #emph[idle drones:] if they take occasion (though never so just) of going abroad, #emph[gadders, and lightfooted.] This bad opinion of their wives is a cause that their hearts are clean removed from their own, and set upon strange flesh: whereby the devil gaineth what he desireth, that is, to put asunder such as God hath joined together, and to join those whom God hath put asunder.

=== §. 11. #emph[Of husbands] #footnote[Treat. 3. §. 7. V. Husbands must entirely delight in their wives.] #emph[entire affection to their wives.]

An husbands #emph[affection] to his wife must be answerable to his #emph[opinion] of her: he ought therefore #emph[to delight in his wife entirely,] that is, so to #emph[delight in her as wholly and only delighting in her:] In this respect the Prophets wife is called the #footnote[#emph[Ezec.] 24. 16.] #emph[desire,] or delight, or pleasure #emph[of his eyes:] that wherein he most of all delighted, and therefore by a propriety so called.

Such delight did #emph[Isaac] take in his wife as it drove out a contrary strong passion, namely the #emph[grief] which he took for the departure of his mother: for it is noted that #emph[he] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 24. 67.] #emph[loved her, and was comforted after his mothers death.]

This kind of affection the #emph[wise-man] doth elegantly set forth in these words, #emph[Rejoice with the wife of thy youth: Let her] #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 5. 18, 19. expounded.] #emph[be as the loving Hind, and pleasant Roe, and be thou ravisht always with her love.] Here note both the #emph[metaphors,] and also the #emph[hyperbole] which are used to set forth an husbands #emph[delight] in his wife. In the #emph[metaphors] again note both the #emph[creatures] whereunto a wife is resembled, and also the attributes given to them. The creatures are two, an #emph[Hind] and a #emph[Roe,] which are the females of an #emph[Hart] and a #emph[Roe-Buck:] now it is noted of the #emph[Hart] and #emph[Roe-Buck,] that of all other beasts they are most inamored (as I may so speak) with their mates, and even mad again in their heat and desire after them.

These metaphors hath #emph[Solomon] used to set forth that unfained and earnest, entire and ardent affection which an husband ought to bear unto his wife: which being taken in a good sense, and rightly applied, so as they exceed not the bonds of christian modesty and decency, are very fit, and pertinent to the purpose: if we stretch them beyond modesty, we wrong the pen-man of them, or rather the Holy Ghost that directed him, and propound a pernicious pattern unto husbands.

The attributes given to the forenamed creatures much amplify the point: the former is termed a #emph[loving] Hind, the latter #footnote[#emph[Col.] 1. 13..] a #emph[pleasant] Roe, word for word an Hind #emph[of Loves,] a Roe #emph[of favour,] that is, exceedingly loved and favoured: (for to set forth the extent of Gods love unto his son, Christ is called #emph[the son of his love:)]

These comparisons applied to a wife, do lively set forth that delight which an husband ought to take in her, and yet is it much further amplified by the #emph[hyperbole] used in this phrase, #emph[be thou ravisht with her love,] word for word #emph[err thou in her love,] by which no sinful error, or dotage is meant, but a lawful earnest affection: implying two things especially: First so far to exceed, as to make a man oversee some such blemishes in his wife, as others would soon espy and mislike: or else to count them no blemishes, delighting in her never a whit the less for them. For example, if a man have a wife, not very beautiful, or proper, but having some deformity in her body, some imperfection in her speech, sight, gesture, or any part of her body, yet so to affect her, and delight in her, as if she were the fairest, and every way most complete woman in the world. Secondly, so highly to esteem, so ardently to affect, so tenderly to respect her, as others may think him even to dote on her. An husbands affection to his wife cannot be too great if it kept within the bonds of honesty, sobriety and comeliness. The wives affection ought to be as great to her husband, yet because of the husbands place of authority, he must especially take all occasions to manifest this his inward affection. Read the Song of Songs, and in it you shall observe such affection manifested by Christ to his Spouse, as would make one think he did (with reverence in an holy manner to use the phrase) even err in his love and dote on her. A good pattern and president for husbands. For nothing is more lovely them a #footnote[#emph[Nihil viro vxore bona amabilius. Hier. in Ezec.] 24.] good wife.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of the Stoical disposition of husbands to their wives.]

Contrary is the disposition of such husbands as have no heat, or heart of affection in them: but Stoick-like delight no more in their own wives then in any other women, nor account them any dearer then others. A disposition no way warranted by the word. The faithful Saints of God before mentioned, as also many other like to them, were no Stoics, without all affection: nor did they think it a matter unbeseeming them after a peculiar manner to delight in their wives (witness #emph[Isaacks sporting with his wife)] for this is a privilege which appertaineth #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 26. 8.] to the estate of marriage. But that I be not mistaken herein, let it be noted that the affection whereof I speak is not a carnal, sensual, beastly affection, but such an one as may stand with Christian gravity and so briety: having relation to the soul of a mans wife as well as to her body, grounded both on the near conjunction of marriage, and also on the inward qualities of his wife.

Thus far of an husbands inward respect of his wife. It followeth to speak of his outward carriage towards her.
